#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be8b47e-8161-5ac6-af15-f112a84dd1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2be8b47e-8161-5ac6-af15-f112a84dd1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315375} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdde61d2-0b02-5fd6-a68f-661c818e9a55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53ed59d8-b22e-538d-9cc1-0f9210effe07" :question/math-academy-id "q-342596" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a9914a1-bd63-5503-bb36-c2533c2c5218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063355} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc67312-c83d-5cab-b917-ee2161d29129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063356} {:answer/id #uuid "c283d4fd-1d67-5c92-a428-3fc3d98830e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063357} {:answer/id #uuid "cad706a5-f7c0-5286-9b96-bf0ba4a525c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063358} {:answer/id #uuid "de134615-356c-50aa-8b5e-53d741c02ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063359}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5881a3d1-5740-518f-bfdd-3ac99c905e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063354}] :question/id #uuid "59090c66-6332-5a45-a658-fc8034750491" :question/math-academy-id "q-5028" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 4x - \\sin x$ and the point $P(0, 0)$ that lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, calculate $(f^{-1})^{′}(0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2576954f-b0ee-571c-85ae-5533e6d22543" :question/math-academy-id "q-75836" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $5$ | $4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=8$ and ${f}^{'}(8)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10dcb0f4-5f0f-5b93-a598-6c0cd2ed4435" :question/math-academy-id "q-76105" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ For $x\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}\\cdot (2x-1) & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{\\cancel{(2x-1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(2x-1)} & =4x-2 \\\\ 3x+1 & =4x-2 \\\\ 1+2 & =4x-3x \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{'}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =-\\frac{5}{(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(3) & =-\\frac{5}{{(2\\cdot 3-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{(5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
